This paper delves into the existence of solutions for a specific class of quasilinear elliptic systems with convective terms, characterized by singular potential and reaction components. The solutions sought are nontrivial and nonnegative, belonging to the Sobolev space $W^{1,p}(R^N)$. The system's form is given by:

\begin{equation*}
\begin{cases}
-\Delta_{p}u+|y|^{a}|\xi|^{b}\Delta_{p}v=\frac{\vartheta}{|y|^{q}}u^{s}v^{-\eta},\\
-\Delta_{p}v+|y|^{a}|\xi|^{b}\Delta_{p}u=\frac{\vartheta}{|y|^{q}}u^{\eta}v^{-s},
\end{cases}
\end{equation*}

where $q, a, b, s, \eta$ are positive constants, and the functions $u$ and $v$ are defined on the domain $D=\{(x,y,\xi)\in R^3\}$. The study also considers the existence of positive solutions under certain conditions, with the aid of the mountain pass theorem and the Ekeland variational principle.
